<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term · script</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grantha script</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Grantha script is a South Indian script derived from Brahmi, used from Pallava times to write <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> in the Tamil country, where the Tamil script lacked letters for many Sanskrit sounds. It was used for Sanskrit texts and inscriptions until the twentieth century, is the ancestor of the Malayalam script, and its Pallava form influenced the early scripts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and the Malay world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grantha-script.jpg" alt="Grantha script" width="320" height="515" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Symphonicallyu · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grantha_aksharamala.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
